### Business Flow

| # | Severity | Issue | Location | Recommendation |
| --- | ---------- | ------- | ---------- | ---------------- |
| 1 | 🔴 CRITICAL | `restore_tool()` extracts archive to target directory without cleaning existing data first. Stale files from a previous installation will persist alongside restored files, causing data integrity risk (e.g. old config files overriding restored ones). | `tools/backup/backup_manager.py` `restore_tool()` L~80 | Add `shutil.rmtree(target)` before `untar()` with a confirmation prompt, or extract to a temp directory and atomically swap. At minimum, log a warning listing pre-existing files. |
| 2 | 🔴 CRITICAL | `backup_tool()` with `dest="gdrive"` calls `gdrive.py upload` via subprocess but does not check the return code. A failed upload silently reports success to the user. | `tools/backup/backup_manager.py` `backup_tool()` L~65 | Capture and check `subprocess.run(...).returncode`. If non-zero, log error and return failure. Add `log_err("Google Drive upload failed")` on failure path. |
| 3 | 🟡 WARNING | `cmd_install()` in `arwaky.py` runs `git submodule update --init` but does not verify the return code before proceeding to per-tool installers. If submodule init fails, all subsequent tool installs will fail with confusing errors. | `tools/cli/arwaky.py` `cmd_install()` L~195 | Check `run_cmd()` return code. If non-zero, log error and abort with actionable message: `"Submodule init failed. Run 'aa submodules' manually."` |
| 4 | 🟡 WARNING | `inject_9router_env()` skips env injection when key is placeholder but only logs a `log_skip()`. The user completes the connect flow believing everything is configured, then discovers at runtime that 9Router calls fail. | `tools/connect/connect.py` `inject_9router_env()` | Elevate to `log_warn()` and add a summary line at the end of connect: `"⚠ 9Router env NOT injected: API key is placeholder. Run 'aa 9router' to configure."` |
| 5 | 🟡 WARNING | `cmd_stop()` in `ninerouter_daemon.py` for the non-systemd path calls `podman stop` but does not verify the container actually stopped before returning success. | `tools/daemons/ninerouter_daemon.py` `cmd_stop()` | After `run([engine, "stop", ...])`, call `container_running()` and verify it returns `False`. If still running after timeout, log error. |
